<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Paris (1814)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-sixth-coalition">War of the Sixth Coalition</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1815">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1814 made peace between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the allied powers after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s first abdication. It restored France roughly to its 1792 borders and returned most of its colonies, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seychelles">Seychelles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-lucia">Saint Lucia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a>. Further European questions were left to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty was signed on 30 May 1814 and ended the war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the Sixth Coalition. It followed an armistice of 23 April between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles of Bourbon</a>, Count of Artois, acting as Lieutenant General of the Realm, and the allies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> had abdicated as Emperor on 6 April after negotiations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a>. Peace talks began on 9 May, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand</a> negotiating on behalf of the exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a>. The allies had defined their common war aim at Chaumont in March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The signatories were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Lords Castlereagh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a> and Cathcart for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, Counts Razumovsky and Nesselrode for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Prince Metternich</a> and Count Stadion for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and Baron Hardenberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-von-humboldt">Wilhelm von Humboldt</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> also signed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ratified in July. The allies concluded separate treaties with France rather than a common document.</p><p>The allies wished to establish a lasting peace and did not think harsh terms necessary now that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had a monarchy again. France kept the borders of 1792 and was allowed to retain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a>, Saarlouis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landau">Landau</a>, the County of Montbéliard, part of Savoy with Annecy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> with the Comtat Venaissin. It also kept the artworks acquired during the war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, and the treaty recognised the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Article 32 called for a congress of all the belligerents within two months, which became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, held from September 1814 to June 1815. The treaty also aimed to end the French slave trade over five years, though not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> itself, and it left French territories outside this aim. Several powers still feared a revival of French power. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, now freed from French rule, accepted William I of the House of Orange as its prince, with the secret support of the great powers.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s brief return to power and his defeat, a second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1815">Treaty of Paris</a> was signed on 20 November 1815. For this reason the treaty of 30 May 1814 is sometimes called the First Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Paris (1814)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The island was ceded in the 1814 <b>Treaty of Paris</b>, renamed Mauritius, and its first governor, Robert Farquhar, ruled from Port Louis.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-mauritius">British Mauritius</a></li><li><blockquote>After the capture of Mauritius in 1810 they passed under British control, confirmed by the <b>Treaty of Paris</b> in 1814, and were governed as a dependency of Mauritius.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-seychelles">British Seychelles</a></li><li><blockquote>After about fourteen changes of ownership between France and Britain, Saint Lucia was finally ceded to Britain by the <b>Treaty of Paris</b> in 1814.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-saint-lucia">Colony of Saint Lucia</a></li><li><blockquote>Britain captured the island again in 1803 and the <b>Treaty of Paris</b> of 1814 ceded it to Britain, creating the Colony of Saint Lucia.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-saint-lucia">French Saint Lucia</a></li><li><blockquote>British forces captured the island in 1810, and the <b>Treaty of Paris</b> of 1814 confirmed its cession along with the Seychelles.</blockquote><a href="/state/isle-de-france">Isle de France</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Paris</b> of 1814 restored Guadeloupe to France.</blockquote><a href="/state/swedish-guadeloupe">Swedish Guadeloupe</a></li></ul></section>
